.TH "MANIFEST.TOML" "5" "" "" "Flox User Manuals"
.SH NAME
manifest.toml \- declarative environment configuration format
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[CR]manifest.toml\f[R] file is a declarative format for specifying
the packages installed in an environment, environment variables to make
available to the environment, a shell script to run upon activation of
the environment, and other options to change the behavior of the
environment.
.SH DESCRIPTION
Flox environments come with a declarative manifest in \f[B]TOML
format\f[R] <\f[I]https://toml.io/en/v1.0.0\f[R]>.
An environment can be defined entirely by this one file.
The file is divided into just a few sections that are represented as
TOML tables:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]\f[CB][install]\f[B]\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]\f[CB][vars]\f[B]\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]\f[CB][hook]\f[B]\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]\f[CB][profile]\f[B]\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]\f[CB][services]\f[B]\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]\f[CB][include]\f[B]\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]\f[CB][build]\f[B]\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]\f[CB][options]\f[B]\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
[\f[CR]containerize\f[R]] \- see
\f[B]\f[CB]flox\-containerize(1)\f[B]\f[R]
.SS \f[CR]schema\-version\f[R]
\f[CR]schema\-version\f[R] is a top\-level field that specifies the
minimum version of Flox that the manifest is compatible with.
.IP
.EX
schema\-version = \(dq1.10.0\(dq
.EE
.PP
Valid string values are:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]1.10.0\f[R]: introduced package outputs
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]1.11.0\f[R]: introduced \f[CR]minimum\-cli\-version\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]1.12.0\f[R]: introduced services \f[CR]auto\-start\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]1.13.0\f[R]: introduced \f[CR]profile.deactivate\f[R] and build
\f[CR]sandbox\-allow\f[R]
.PP
Existing manifest schemas, including the older \f[CR]version = 1\f[R]
format, are automatically forward\-migrated when using features that
require a newer schema (e.g.\ \f[CR]flox install\f[R] with package
outputs).
.SS \f[CR]minimum\-cli\-version\f[R]
\f[CR]minimum\-cli\-version\f[R] is a top\-level field that specifies
the minimum version of the Flox CLI required to use the environment.
Using an older version will emit a warning but not block the command.
This is useful when an environment depends on behavior introduced in a
specific release (e.g.\ a bugfix or feature that isn\(cqt schema
related).
.IP
.EX
minimum\-cli\-version = \(dq1.11.0\(dq
.EE
.PP
The value must be a valid \f[B]semantic
version\f[R] <\f[I]https://semver.org/\f[R]> string
(e.g.\ \f[CR]\(dq1.11.0\(dq\f[R]).
Invalid values cause the manifest to be rejected.
.PP
An optional \f[CR]reason\f[R] can be provided using the table form which
will be included in the emitted warning message:
.IP
.EX
\f[B][minimum\-cli\-version]\f[R]
version = \(dq1.11.0\(dq
reason = \(dqNeeds feature X\(dq
.EE
.SS \f[CR][install]\f[R]
The \f[CR][install]\f[R] table is the core of the environment,
specifying which packages you\(cqd like installed in the environment.
An example of the \f[CR][install]\f[R] table is shown below:
.IP
.EX
\f[B][install]\f[R]
ripgrep.pkg\-path = \(dqripgrep\(dq
pip.pkg\-path = \(dqpython310Packages.pip\(dq
.EE
.PP
Since this is TOML, equivalent ways of writing this would be
.IP
.EX
\f[B][install]\f[R]
ripgrep = { pkg\-path = \(dqripgrep\(dq }
pip = { pkg\-path = \(dqpython310Packages.pip\(dq }
.EE
.PP
or
.IP
.EX
\f[B][install.ripgrep]\f[R]
pkg\-path = \(dqripgrep\(dq

\f[B][install.pip]\f[R]
pkg\-path = \(dqpython310Packages.pip\(dq
.EE
.PP
Flox will use the first format by default when automatically editing the
manifest.
.SS Package names
Packages are organized in a hierarchical structure such that certain
packages are found at the top level (e.g.\ \f[CR]ripgrep\f[R]), and
other packages are found under package sets
(e.g.\ \f[CR]python310Packages.pip\f[R]).
We call this location within the catalog the \(lqpkg\-path\(rq.
.PP
The pkg\-path is searched when you execute a \f[CR]flox search\f[R]
command.
The pkg\-path is what\(cqs shown by \f[CR]flox show\f[R].
Finally, the pkg\-path appears in your manifest after a
\f[CR]flox install\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\f[B][install]\f[R]
ripgrep.pkg\-path = \(dqripgrep\(dq
pip.pkg\-path = \(dqpython310Packages.pip\(dq
.EE
.SS Package descriptors
Each entry in the \f[CR][install]\f[R] table is a key\-value pair.
The key in the key\-value pair (e.g.\ \f[CR]ripgrep\f[R],
\f[CR]pip\f[R]) is referred to as an \(lqinstall ID\(rq, and represents
the name by which you will refer to a particular package e.g.
if you wanted to uninstall or upgrade the package.
Install IDs are inferred from the last attribute in the pkg\-path, but
may also be specified either at install\-time via the \f[CR]\-i\f[R]
option or interactively via \f[B]\f[CB]flox\-edit(1)\f[B]\f[R].
.PP
The value in the key\-value pair is called a \(lqpackage descriptor\(rq.
A package is specified by a number of available options which are
separate from the install ID, so you are free to change them
independently of one another.
This allows you to change package details while keeping a stable install
ID, for example upgrading from \f[CR]gcc.pkg\-path = \(dqgcc12\(dq\f[R]
to \f[CR]gcc.pkg\-path = \(dqgcc13\(dq\f[R].
.PP
Most package descriptors will be catalog descriptors, which allow
specifying packages from the Flox catalog.
A second format, flake descriptors, is also supported, which allows
specifying software to install from an arbitrary Nix flake.
.SS Catalog descriptors
The full list of catalog descriptor options is:
.IP
.EX
Descriptor ::= {
  pkg\-group          = null | <STRING>
, version            = null | <STRING>
, systems            = null | [<STRING>, ...]
, pkg\-path           = <STRING>
, priority           = null | <INT>
, outputs            = null | \(dqall\(dq | [<STRING>, ...]
}
.EE
.PP
Only \f[CR]pkg\-path\f[R] is required.
.PP
By specifying some of these options you create a set of requirements
that the installed program must satisfy, otherwise installation will
fail.
.PP
By default, all packages belong to the same \f[CR]pkg\-group\f[R], which
means providing specific versions for two different packages can quickly
lead to installation failures.
To avoid such failures, either give a looser \f[CR]version\f[R]
constraint, or move one of the packages to a different package group.
.PP
Each option is described below:
.TP
\f[CR]pkg\-group\f[R]
Marks a package as belonging to a pkg\-group.
.RS
.PP
The pkg\-group is a collection of software that is known to work
together at a point in time.
Adding packages to a pkg\-group enables packages in the pkg\-group to
share the same libraries and dependencies, which ensures maximum
compatibility and minimizes the size of the environment.
.PP
Packages are marked as belonging to a pkg\-group simply by setting this
option to the name of the pkg\-group.
Packages that do not have a pkg\-group specified belong to the same
group.
.PP
Multiple pkg\-groups may resolve to the same version of the catalog.
Pkg\-groups are upgraded as a unit, ensuring that the packages within
the pkg\-group continue to work together.
See \f[B]\f[CB]flox\-upgrade(1)\f[B]\f[R] for more details on how
pkg\-groups and packages interact during upgrades.
.RE
.TP
\f[CR]version\f[R]
Requires that the package match either an exact version or a semver
range.
.RS
.PP
The semantic version can be specified with the typical qualifiers such
as \f[CR]\(ha\f[R], \f[CR]>=\f[R], etc.
Semantic versions that do not specify all three fields
(\f[CR]MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH\f[R]) will treat the unspecified fields as
wildcards.
This instructs Flox to find the latest versions for those fields.
For example \f[CR]version = \(dq1.2\(dq\f[R] would select the latest
version in the \f[CR]1.2.X\f[R] series.
.RE
.TP
\f[CR]systems\f[R]
A list of systems on which to install this package.
When omitted this defaults to the same systems that the manifest
specifies that it supports via \f[CR]options.systems\f[R].
.TP
\f[CR]pkg\-path\f[R]
The abbreviated location of a package within a catalog.
A pkg\-path is a sequence of one or more attributes joined by a
delimiter.
For example, both \f[CR]ripgrep\f[R] and
\f[CR]python310Packages.pip\f[R] are pkg\-paths.
A pkg\-path that contains more than one attribute can be represented as
either a single string that contains a `.'\-delimited sequence of the
attributes, or it can be represented as a TOML array of strings where
each string is an attribute.
For example, both \f[CR]\(dqpython310Packages.pip\(dq\f[R] and
\f[CR][\(dqpython310Packages\(dq, \(dqpip\(dq]\f[R] are equivalent for
the \f[CR]pkg\-path\f[R] option.
.TP
\f[CR]priority\f[R]
A priority used to resolve file conflicts where lower values indicate
higher priority.
.RS
.PP
Each package internally has \f[CR]/bin\f[R], \f[CR]/man\f[R],
\f[CR]/include\f[R], and other directories for the files they provide.
These directories from all packages in the environment are merged when
building the environment.
Two packages that provide the same \f[CR]/bin/foo\f[R] file cause a
conflict, and it\(cqs ambiguous which file should ultimately be placed
into the environment.
Such conflicts can be resolved by assigning different priorities to the
conflicting packages.
.PP
The default priority is 5.
Packages with a lower \f[CR]priority\f[R] value will take precedence
over packages with higher \f[CR]priority\f[R] values.
.RE
.TP
\f[CR]outputs\f[R]
Selects which outputs of a package to install.
.RS
.PP
Packages can split up the files they install into what are called
outputs.
For example, a package can put binaries into a \f[CR]bin\f[R] output and
development headers in a \f[CR]dev\f[R] output.
If an environment only needs to use the binaries from the package, it
can reduce its size by only installing the \f[CR]bin\f[R] output.
.PP
The outputs of existing packages can be inspected with
\f[CR]flox list \-a\f[R] and new packages can be inspected with
\f[CR]flox show\f[R].
.PP
When omitted, the package\(cqs default outputs are installed.
When set to an array of strings
(e.g.\ \f[CR][\(dqbin\(dq, \(dqman\(dq]\f[R]), only those specific
outputs are installed.
When set to the string \f[CR]\(dqall\(dq\f[R], all available outputs of
the package are installed.
.RE
.SS Flake descriptors
Flake descriptors allow installing software from an arbitrary Nix flake.
.PP
The full list of flake descriptor options is:
.IP
.EX
Descriptor ::= {
  flake              = <STRING>
, systems            = null | [<STRING>, ...]
, priority           = null | <INT>
, outputs            = null | \(dqall\(dq | [<STRING>, ...]
}
.EE
.PP
Only \f[CR]flake\f[R] is required.
\f[CR]systems\f[R], \f[CR]priority\f[R], and \f[CR]outputs\f[R] behave
the same as described above for catalog descriptors, and
\f[CR]flake\f[R] is described below:
.TP
\f[CR]flake\f[R]
Specifies a Nix flake installable, which Nix refers to as a flake output
attribute and documents at
https://nix.dev/manual/nix/2.17/command\-ref/new\-cli/nix#flake\-output\-attribute.
Flake installables are of the form \f[CR]flakeref[#attrpath]\f[R], where
flakeref is a flake reference and attrpath is an optional attribute
path.
.RS
.PP
Flox tries to use the same fallback behavior as Nix; if no attrpath is
specified, the flake is checked for containing
\f[CR]packages.$system.default\f[R] or
\f[CR]defaultPackage.$system\f[R].
If an attrpath is specified, it is checked whether
\f[CR]packages.$system.$attrpath\f[R] or
\f[CR]legacyPackages.$system.$attrpath\f[R] exist.
.RE
.SS Store paths
Store path descriptors allow installing software from an arbitrary Nix
store path.
.PP
The full list of store path descriptor options is:
.IP
.EX
Descriptor ::= {
  store\-path         = STRING
, systems            = null | [<STRING>, ...]
, priority           = null | <INT>
}
.EE
.PP
Only \f[CR]store\-path\f[R] is required.
\f[CR]priority\f[R] behaves the same as described above for catalog
descriptors and flake installables, and \f[CR]store\-path\f[R] is
described below:
.TP
\f[CR]store\-path\f[R]
Specifies a nix store path, i.e.\ a nix built package in
\f[CR]/nix/store\f[R].
This can be the result of a native Nix operations such as
\f[CR]nix build\f[R], \f[CR]nix copy\f[R], etc.
The store path has to be available on the current system in order to
build the environment.
The environment will fail to build on other systems without first
distributing the store path via Nix tooling.
As such, this feature is most suitable for local experiments and ad\-hoc
interoperability with Nix.
.TP
\f[CR]system\f[R]
Behaves equally to the system attribute of catalog descriptors and
flakes installables.
Unlike the former, users are encouraged to specify it, because store
paths are generally system dependent.
.SS \f[CR][vars]\f[R]
The \f[CR][vars]\f[R] section allows you to define environment variables
for your environment that are set during environment activation.
The environment variables specified here cannot reference one another.
The names and values of the environment variables are copied verbatim
into the activation script, so capitalization will be preserved.
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
\f[B][vars]\f[R]
DB_URL = \(dqhttp://localhost:2000\(dq
SERVER_PORT = \(dq3000\(dq
.EE
.SS \f[CR][hook]\f[R]
The \f[CR]on\-activate\f[R] script in the \f[CR][hook]\f[R] section is
useful for performing initialization in a predictable Bash shell
environment.
.SS \f[CR]on\-activate\f[R]
The \f[CR]on\-activate\f[R] script is sourced from a \f[B]bash\f[R]
shell, and it can be useful for spawning processes, dynamically setting
environment variables, and creating files and directories to be used by
the subsequent profile scripts, commands, and shells.
.PP
Hook scripts inherit environment variables set in the \f[CR][vars]\f[R]
section, and variables set here will in turn be inherited by the
\f[CR][profile]\f[R] scripts described below.
.PP
Any output written to \f[CR]stdout\f[R] in a hook script is redirected
to \f[CR]stderr\f[R] to avoid it being mixed with the output of profile
section scripts that write to \f[CR]stdout\f[R] for \(lqin\-place\(rq
activations.
.IP
.EX
\f[B][hook]\f[R]
on\-activate = \(dq\(dq\(dq
    # Interact with the tty as you would in any script
    echo \(dqStarting up $FLOX_ENV_DESCRIPTION environment ...\(dq
    read \-e \-p \(dqFavourite colour or favorite color? \(dq value

    # Set variables, create files and directories
    venv_dir=\(dq$(mktemp \-d)\(dq
    export venv_dir

    # Perform initialization steps, e.g. create a python venv
    python \-m venv \(dq$venv_dir\(dq

    # Invoke apps that configure the environment via stdout
    eval \(dq$(ssh\-agent)\(dq
\(dq\(dq\(dq
.EE
.PP
The \f[CR]on\-activate\f[R] script is not re\-run when multiple
activations are run at the same time; for instance, if
\f[CR]flox activate\f[R] is run in two different shells, the first
activation will run the hook, but the second will not.
After all activations exit, the next \f[CR]flox activate\f[R] will once
again run the hook.
Currently, environment variables set by the first run of the
\f[CR]on\-activate\f[R] script are captured and then set by activations
that don\(cqt run \f[CR]on\-activate\f[R], but this behavior may change.
.PP
The \f[CR]on\-activate\f[R] script may be re\-run by other
\f[CR]flox\f[R] commands; we may create ephemeral activations and thus
run the script multiple times for commands such as
\f[CR]services start\f[R].
For this reason, it\(cqs best practice to make \f[CR]on\-activate\f[R]
idempotent.
However, the environment of your current shell is only affected by the
initial run of the script for the first activation for your shell.
.PP
It\(cqs also best practice to write hooks defensively, assuming the user
is using the environment from any directory on their machine.
.SS \f[CR]script\f[R] \- DEPRECATED
This field was deprecated in favor of the \f[CR]profile\f[R] section.
.SS \f[CR][profile]\f[R]
Scripts defined in the \f[CR][profile]\f[R] section are sourced by
\f[I]your shell\f[R] and inherit environment variables set in the
\f[CR][vars]\f[R] section and by the \f[CR][hook]\f[R] scripts.
The \f[CR]profile.common\f[R] script is sourced for every shell, and
special care should be taken to ensure compatibility with all shells,
after which exactly one of \f[CR]profile.{bash,fish,tcsh,zsh}\f[R] is
sourced by the corresponding shell.
.PP
These scripts are useful for performing shell\-specific customizations
such as setting aliases or configuring the prompt.
.IP
.EX
\f[B][profile]\f[R]
common = \(dq\(dq\(dq
    echo \(dqit\(aqs gettin\(aq flox in here\(dq
\(dq\(dq\(dq
bash = \(dq\(dq\(dq
    source $venv_dir/bin/activate
    alias foo=\(dqecho bar\(dq
    set \-o vi
\(dq\(dq\(dq
zsh = \(dq\(dq\(dq
    source $venv_dir/bin/activate
    alias foo=\(dqecho bar\(dq
    bindkey \-v
\(dq\(dq\(dq
fish = \(dq\(dq\(dq
    source $venv_dir/bin/activate.fish
    alias foo=\(dqecho bar\(dq
    fish_vi_key_bindings
\(dq\(dq\(dq
.EE
.PP
Profile scripts are re\-run when an environment is already active and
\f[CR]eval \(dq$(flox activate)\(dq\f[R] or
\f[CR]flox activate \-c CMD\f[R] is run.
Re\-running profile scripts allows aliases to be set in subshells that
inherit from a parent shell with an already active environment.
.SS \f[CR][services]\f[R]
The \f[CR][services]\f[R] section of the manifest allows you to describe
the services you would like to run as part of your environment e.g.\ a
web server or a database.
The services you define here use the packages provided by the
\f[CR][install]\f[R] section and any variables you\(cqve defined in the
\f[CR][vars]\f[R] section or \f[CR]hook.on\-activate\f[R] script.
.PP
The \f[CR][services]\f[R] section is a table of key\-value pairs where
the keys determine the service names, and the values (service
descriptors) determine how to configure and run the services.
.PP
An example service definition is shown below:
.IP
.EX
\f[B][services.database]\f[R]
command = \(dqpostgres start\(dq
vars.PGUSER = \(dqmyuser\(dq
vars.PGPASSWORD = \(dqsuper\-secret\(dq
vars.PGDATABASE = \(dqmydb\(dq
vars.PGPORT = \(dq9001\(dq
.EE
.PP
This would define a service called \f[CR]database\f[R] that configures
and starts a PostgreSQL database.
.PP
Top\-level options for the \f[CR][services]\f[R] block are:
.TP
\f[CR]auto\-start\f[R]
Whether to start all services automatically on \f[CR]flox activate\f[R].
Can be suppressed with \f[CR]\-\-no\-start\-services\f[R].
.PP
The full set of options for an individual service descriptor is:
.IP
.EX
ServiceDescriptor ::= {
  command    = STRING
, vars       = null | Map[STRING, STRING]
, is\-daemon  = null | BOOL
, shutdown   = null | Shutdown
, systems    = null | [<STRING>, ...]
}

Shutdown ::= {
  command = STRING
}
.EE
.TP
\f[CR]command\f[R]
The command to run (interpreted by a Bash shell) to start the service.
This command can use any environment variables that were set in the
\f[CR][vars]\f[R] section, the \f[CR]hook.on\-activate\f[R] script, or
the service\-specific \f[CR]vars\f[R] table.
.TP
\f[CR]vars\f[R]
A table of environment variables to set for the invocation of this
specific service.
Nothing outside of this service will observe these environment
variables.
.TP
\f[CR]is\-daemon\f[R]
Whether this service spawns a daemon when it starts.
Some commands start a background process and then terminate instead of
themselves running for an extended period of time.
The underlying process manager cannot track the PID of the daemon that
is spawned, only the PID of the process that \f[I]spawned\f[R] the
daemon.
For this reason you must set the \f[CR]is\-daemon\f[R] option to
\f[CR]true\f[R], otherwise \f[CR]flox services status\f[R] will show
that the service has terminated even though the daemon may still be
running.
Furthermore, since the process manager doesn\(cqt know the PID of the
daemon itself, it cannot deliver a shutdown signal to the daemon.
For this reason you must \f[I]also\f[R] provide the
\f[CR]shutdown.command\f[R] option so that the process manager knows
what command to run to shut down the daemon.
Failure to set both \f[CR]is\-daemon\f[R] and
\f[CR]shutdown.command\f[R] will allow the daemon to continue running
even after running \f[CR]flox services stop\f[R] or exiting the last
activation of the environment.
.TP
\f[CR]shutdown.command\f[R]
A command to run to shut down the service instead of delivering the
SIGTERM signal to the process.
Some programs require special handling to shut down properly e.g.\ a
program that spawns a server process and uses a client to tell the
server to shut down.
Sending a SIGTERM to a client in that case may not shut down the server.
In those cases you may provide a specific shutdown command to run
instead of relying on the default behavior of sending a SIGTERM to the
service.
This field is required if the \f[CR]is\-daemon\f[R] field is
\f[CR]true\f[R].
.TP
\f[CR]systems\f[R]
An optional list of systems on which to run this service.
If omitted, the service is not restricted.
.SS \f[CR][include]\f[R]
The \f[CR][include]\f[R] section of the manifest describes other
environments that you\(cqd like to merge with the current manifest in
order to compose them into a single environment.
.PP
The list of environments to include is specified by the
\f[CR]include.environments\f[R] array.
The order of the \(lqinclude descriptors\(rq in this array specifies the
priority that should be used when merging the manifests.
Descriptors later in the array take higher priority than those earlier
in the array, and manifest fields in the composing manifest take the
highest priority.
.PP
The merged manifest can be viewed with \f[CR]flox list \-\-config\f[R].
.SS Syntax
An example \f[CR][include]\f[R] section is shown below:
.IP
.EX
\f[B][include]\f[R]
environments = [
    { dir = \(dq../path/to/env\(dq },
    { dir = \(dq../path/to/other/env\(dq, name = \(dqmyenv\(dq }
]
.EE
.PP
As mentioned above, you include other environments by listing them as an
array of tables in the \f[CR]include.environments\f[R] array.
The schema for these \(lqinclude descriptors\(rq is shown below:
.IP
.EX
IncludeDescriptor ::= LocalIncludeDescriptor
                    | FloxHubIncludeDescriptor
                    | RemoteIncludeDescriptor (deprecated)

LocalIncludeDescriptor ::= {
  dir  = STRING
, name = null | STRING
}

FloxHubIncludeDescriptor ::= {
  remote = STRING
, name   = null | STRING
}

# Deprecated, will be removed in a future release
RemoteIncludeDescriptor ::= {
  remote = STRING
, name   = null | STRING
}
.EE
.PP
The fields in these include descriptors are as follows:
.TP
\f[CR]dir\f[R]
The local path to the environment to include.
This has the same semantics as the \f[CR]\-\-dir\f[R] flag passed to
many Flox commands.
.TP
\f[CR]remote\f[R]
The FloxHub reference of an environment to include.
This has the same semantics as the
\f[CR]\-\-reference <env\-ref> \-\-upstream\f[R] flag passed to many
Flox commands.
That is, Flox will include the referenced environment as it is stored on
FloxHub.
.TP
\f[CR]name\f[R]
An optional override to the name of the included environment.
This is useful when you are including multiple environments that have
the same name, or when you want to provide a more convenient name for
the included environment.
.PP
Changes to the included environments aren\(cqt automatically reflected
in the composing environment.
You control when updates are pulled in by using
\f[B]\f[CB]flox include upgrade\f[B]\f[R].
.SS Merge semantics
When merging manifests, different sections have different merge
semantics.
As mentioned above, the order in which include descriptors are listed in
the \f[CR]include.environments\f[R] array determines the priority of the
manifests, with the composing manifest having the highest priority.
In the following discussion we refer to \(lqlower priority manifests\(rq
and \(lqhigher priority manifests\(rq as those being listed earlier or
later in the array, respectively.
.PP
As of right now there is no way to \f[I]remove\f[R] something from a
lower priority manifest, but things can be overridden or added by higher
priority manifests.
.TP
\f[CR][install]\f[R]
Package descriptors are overwritten entirely by a higher priority
manifest.
.TP
\f[CR][vars]\f[R]
Variables are overwritten entirely by a higher priority manifest.
.TP
\f[CR][hook]\f[R]
The scripts in \f[CR]hook\f[R] are appended to one another with a
newline in between.
Scripts from higher priority manifests come after those from lower
priority manifests.
.TP
\f[CR][profile]\f[R]
The scripts in the \f[CR]profile\f[R] section are appended in the same
way that they are for \f[CR]hook\f[R].
.TP
\f[CR][services]\f[R]
Service descriptors are entirely overwritten by higher priority
manifests
.TP
\f[CR][include]\f[R]
The \f[CR]include\f[R] section is omitted from merged manifests, so no
merging of the \f[CR]include\f[R] section ever happens.
.TP
\f[CR][containerize]\f[R]
The \f[CR]containerize.config\f[R] field is deep merged, meaning that
individual fields of \f[CR]containerize.config\f[R] are merged rather
than \f[CR]containerize.config\f[R] being completely overwritten.
The fields within \f[CR]containerize.config\f[R] are merged as follows:
\f[CR]user\f[R], \f[CR]cmd\f[R], \f[CR]working_dir\f[R], and
\f[CR]stop_signal\f[R] are overwritten; \f[CR]labels\f[R] and
\f[CR]exposed_ports\f[R] are merged via the union of the values in the
high priority and low priority manifests.
.TP
\f[CR][options]\f[R]
The \f[CR]options\f[R] section is also deep merged, meaning that
individual fields of the \f[CR]options\f[R] section are merged rather
than being completely overwritten.
All of the fields in the \f[CR]options\f[R] section are individually
overwritten by higher priority manifests
e.g.\ \f[CR]options.allow.broken\f[R] is individually overwritten by a
higher priority manifest, as is \f[CR]options.allow.licenses\f[R], etc.
.PP
This has implications for the activation mode of a composed environment.
Since the default activation mode is \f[CR]dev\f[R], it is not present
in the manifest by default.
This means that if one included environment sets
\f[CR]options.activate.mode\f[R] to \f[CR]run\f[R], the merged manifest
will also have \f[CR]options.activate.mode = run\f[R] unless a higher
priority manifest explicitly sets
\f[CR]options.activate.mode = dev\f[R].
.SS \f[CR][build]\f[R]
The \f[CR][build]\f[R] section of the manifest allows you to describe
build instructions for custom packages that can be built using
\f[CR]flox build\f[R].
.PP
The build commands use the packages provided by the \f[CR][install]\f[R]
section and any variables defined in the \f[CR][vars]\f[R] section or
\f[CR]hook.on\-activate\f[R] script.
.PP
The \f[CR][build]\f[R] section is a table of key\-value pairs where the
keys determine the package names, and the values (build descriptors)
determine how to build the package.
.PP
An example build definition is shown below:
.IP
.EX
\f[B][build.hello]\f[R]
command = \(aq\(aq\(aq
mkdir \-p $out
echo \(aqHello, World!\(aq >> $out/hello.txt
\(aq\(aq\(aq
.EE
.PP
This would define a package called \f[CR]hello\f[R] that produces a
single file \f[CR]hello.txt\f[R] containing the string
\f[CR]\(dqHello, World!\(dq\f[R].
See \f[CR]flox\-build(1)\f[R] for more details.
.PP
The full set of options is shown below:
.IP
.EX
BuildDescriptor ::= {
  command          = STRING
, sandbox          = null | (\(dqoff\(dq | \(dqwarn\(dq | \(dqenforce\(dq | \(dqpure\(dq)
, sandbox\-allow    = null | [<STRING>, ...]
, version          = null | STRING | VersionFile | VersionCommand
, description      = null | STRING
, runtime\-packages = null | [<STRING>, ...]
}

VersionFile ::= {
  file = STRING
}

VersionCommand ::= {
  command = STRING
}
.EE
.TP
\f[CR]command\f[R]
The command to run (interpreted by a Bash shell) to build the package.
This command can use any environment variables that were set in the
\f[CR][vars]\f[R] section, or the \f[CR]hook.on\-activate\f[R] script.
.TP
\f[CR]sandbox\f[R]
The level of sandboxing applied to the build.
When set to \f[CR]\(dqoff\(dq\f[R], the build is executed in a subshell
of the current shell and is similar to running the commands manually in
a subshell created by \f[CR]flox activate\f[R].
When set to \f[CR]\(dqwarn\(dq\f[R], the build runs as for
\f[CR]\(dqoff\(dq\f[R], but a warning is printed for each file the build
accesses from outside its package closure.
This helps surface undeclared dependencies without failing the build.
When set to \f[CR]\(dqenforce\(dq\f[R], the build runs as for
\f[CR]\(dqwarn\(dq\f[R], but access to files outside the build\(cqs
package closure is denied, so a build that relies on an undeclared
dependency fails.
When set to \f[CR]\(dqpure\(dq\f[R], the build will be unable to make
network connections and can only access to the files currently under
version control.
Consequently, \f[CR]\(dqpure\(dq\f[R] builds require your project to be
under \f[CR]git\f[R] version control.
.TP
\f[CR]sandbox\-allow\f[R]
A list of paths or glob patterns the build is permitted to read from
outside its package closure without the
\f[CR]\(dqwarn\(dq\f[R]/\f[CR]\(dqenforce\(dq\f[R] sandbox warning about
them (and, under \f[CR]\(dqenforce\(dq\f[R], without failing the build).
A leading \f[CR]\(ti/\f[R] is expanded to the user\(cqs home directory,
and \f[CR]*\f[R]/\f[CR]**\f[R] match across path separators, so for
example \f[CR]\(dq\(ti/.npm/**\(dq\f[R] allows any file under
\f[CR]\(ti/.npm\f[R].
Individual patterns must not contain spaces (the list is passed to the
sandbox as a space\-separated value).
Only meaningful for the local sandbox modes (\f[CR]\(dqwarn\(dq\f[R] and
\f[CR]\(dqenforce\(dq\f[R]); it has no effect under
\f[CR]\(dqoff\(dq\f[R] or \f[CR]\(dqpure\(dq\f[R].
.TP
\f[CR]version\f[R]
The version to attach to this build artifact.
This may be specified in one of the following ways:
.RS
.IP "1." 3
\f[B]as a string\f[R]: \f[CR]version = \(dq0.0.1\(dq\f[R]
.IP "2." 3
\f[B]as read from a file\f[R]: \f[CR]version.file = \(dq<path>\(dq\f[R]
.IP "3." 3
\f[B]as returned by a command\f[R]:
\f[CR]version.command = \(dq<cmd> <args>\(dq\f[R]
.RE
.TP
\f[CR]description\f[R]
The description string to attach to this build artifact.
.TP
\f[CR]runtime\-packages\f[R]
A list of install IDs from the \f[CR][install]\f[R] section of the
manifest, used to restrict what dependencies are provided to the built
package at runtime.
By default all packages from the default package group are included as
runtime dependencies.
If this option is specified, only the listed packages are included.
.SS \f[CR][options]\f[R]
The \f[CR][options]\f[R] section of the manifest details settings for
the environment itself.
.PP
The full set of options are listed below:
.IP
.EX
Options ::= {
  systems                   = null | [<STRING>, ...]
, activate                  = null | Activate
, allow                     = null | Allows
, semver                    = null | Semver
, cuda\-detection            = null | <BOOL>
}

Activate ::= {
  mode = null | \(aqdev\(aq | \(aqrun\(aq
}

Allows ::= {
  unfree   = null | <BOOL>
, broken   = null | <BOOL>
, licenses = null | [<STRING>, ...]
}

Semver ::= {
  allow\-pre\-releases = <BOOL>
}
.EE
.TP
\f[CR]systems\f[R]
The allowlist of systems that this environment supports.
Valid values are \f[CR]x86_64\-linux\f[R], \f[CR]aarch64\-linux\f[R],
\f[CR]x86_64\-darwin\f[R], and \f[CR]aarch64\-darwin\f[R].
\f[B]\f[CB]flox init\f[B]\f[R] automatically populates this list with
the current system type.
A user that attempts to pull an environment from FloxHub when their
environment isn\(cqt explicitly supported will be prompted whether to
automatically add their system to this list.
See \f[B]\f[CB]flox\-pull(1)\f[B]\f[R] for more details.
.TP
\f[CR]activate.mode\f[R]
Whether to activate in \(lqdev\(rq (default) or \(lqrun\(rq mode.
This value can be overridden with \f[CR]flox activate \-\-mode\f[R].
.RS
.PP
In \(lqdev\(rq mode a package, all of its development dependencies, and
language specific environment variables are made available.
As the name implies, this is useful at development time.
However, this may cause unexpected failures when layering environments
or when activating an environment system\-wide.
.PP
In \(lqrun\(rq mode only the requested packages are made available in
\f[CR]PATH\f[R] (and their man pages made available).
This behavior is more in line with what you would expect from a
system\-wide package manager like \f[CR]apt\f[R], \f[CR]yum\f[R], or
\f[CR]brew\f[R].
.RE
.TP
\f[CR]allow.unfree\f[R]
Allows packages with unfree licenses to be installed and appear in
search results.
The default is \f[CR]false\f[R].
.TP
\f[CR]allow.broken\f[R]
Allows packages that are marked \f[CR]broken\f[R] in the catalog to be
installed and appear in search results.
The default is \f[CR]false\f[R].
.TP
\f[CR]allow.licenses\f[R]
An allowlist of software licenses to allow in search results in
installs.
Valid entries are \f[B]SPDX
Identifiers\f[R] <\f[I]https://spdx.org/licenses\f[R]>.
An empty list allows all licenses.
.TP
\f[CR]semver.allow\-pre\-releases\f[R]
Whether to allow pre\-release software for package installations.
The default is \f[CR]false\f[R].
Setting this value to \f[CR]true\f[R] would allow a package version
\f[CR]4.2.0\-pre\f[R] rather than \f[CR]4.1.9\f[R].
.TP
\f[CR]cuda\-detection\f[R]
Whether to detect CUDA libraries and provide them to the environment.
The default is \f[CR]true\f[R].
When enabled, Flox will detect if you have an Nvidia device and attempt
to locate \f[CR]libcuda\f[R] in well\-known paths.
.SH SEE ALSO
